@startuml libhtttp_planned_message_flow

!theme plain

skinparam backgroundColor #FFF9FC
skinparam defaultFontName "Avenir Next"
skinparam defaultFontSize 15
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundcorner 14
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #756A83
  ArrowThickness 1.6
  ParticipantBorderColor #8B7E9F
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  ParticipantFontColor #40364D
  LifeLineBorderColor #C8BFD3
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #F3EDF7
  NoteBorderColor #9FC5A7
  NoteBackgroundColor #E8F5E9
  NoteFontColor #3F6848
  DividerBackgroundColor #F0E7FA
  DividerBorderColor #B69ACF
  DividerFontColor #604978
}

title <size:22><b>Planned HTTTP Message Flow</b></size>\n<size:14>One-shot plaintext parsing above the secure session</size>

participant "Client\n(tetrisu)" as Client #DDF3F5
participant "Protocol\n(libhtttp client)" as ClientHTTP #DFF3E4
participant "Secure Session\n(libtetrissh + TCP)" as Secure #FFF0C9
participant "Server\n(tetrisd)" as Server #F9E2DC
participant "Protocol\n(libhtttp server)" as ServerHTTP #E9E2F7

note over ClientHTTP, ServerHTTP
Planned boundary: libhtttp handles plaintext only.
The caller owns all socket, encryption, and game-state work.
end note

== 1. Client sends a request ==

Client -> ClientHTTP: Structured request
ClientHTTP -> ClientHTTP: Add Content-Length and serialise\nHTTTP/1.0 with CRLF lines
ClientHTTP --> Client: Plaintext byte buffer
Client -> Secure: session_send(plaintext)
Secure -> Secure: Frame, encrypt, transport,\ndecrypt, and de-frame
Secure --> Server: session_recv() returns one complete message
Server -> ServerHTTP: htttp_parse(plaintext)
ServerHTTP --> Server: Structured message or parse error
Server -> ServerHTTP: htttp_dispatch(message)
ServerHTTP --> Server: Invoke registered handler
Server -> Server: Validate and perform application action

note over Server, ServerHTTP #FFF3CD
Malformed grammar or CRLF becomes 400.
Authentication, permissions, room state, and rate limits
remain daemon responsibilities.
end note

== 2. Server returns a response ==

Server -> ServerHTTP: Structured response
ServerHTTP -> ServerHTTP: Add Date and Content-Length,\nthen serialise
ServerHTTP --> Server: Plaintext byte buffer
Server -> Secure: session_send(plaintext)
Secure -> Secure: Encrypt, frame, transport,\ndecrypt, and de-frame
Secure --> Client: session_recv() returns one complete message
Client -> ClientHTTP: htttp_parse(plaintext)
ClientHTTP --> Client: Structured response

== 3. Server may push STATE at any time ==

Server -> ServerHTTP: Structured STATE message
ServerHTTP --> Server: Serialised plaintext
Server -> Secure: session_send(plaintext)
Secure --> Client: Complete decrypted STATE frame
Client -> ClientHTTP: Parse STATE
ClientHTTP --> Client: Structured authoritative state
Client -> Client: Route to renderer

note over Client, Server #FFF3CD
STATE is unprompted and may arrive between ordinary responses,
so the client read loop cannot assume one request means one reply.
end note

@enduml
